\subsection{Reduced drift--distortion interpretation}
\label{sec:riccati-mis-specified-pseudo}

The Riccati analysis above describes how a fictitious $S=0$ observation changes
the estimator covariance.  It does not yet price the fact that the observation
is physically false: the true wave process has nonzero $S$ content, and a
stronger regularizer therefore rejects some desired low-frequency motion.  A
useful reduced model makes this missing cost explicit.

At frequencies below the posterior acceleration correlation rate, approximate
the residual acceleration error by white noise of zero-frequency intensity
$q_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and let the continuous-equivalent $S$ correction have
regularization bandwidth
\begin{equation}
\omega_R=\left(\frac{q_{\mathrm{eff}}}{R_ST_S}\right)^{1/6}.
\label{eq:reduced-omega-r}
\end{equation}
For a wave spectrum sufficiently separated above that correction bandwidth, the
position mean-square error has the leading form
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
J_{\mathrm{red}}(\omega_R)
\simeq
\frac{3q_{\mathrm{eff}}}{2\omega_R^3}
+4m_{-4}\omega_R^4,}
\qquad
m_{-4}:=\int_{\Omega_{\mathrm{wave}}}
\frac{S_p(\omega)}{\omega^4}\,d\omega .
\label{eq:reduced-mse-tradeoff}
\end{equation}
The first term is residual-integrator drift; the second is distortion of the
real wave motion by the false zero observation.  The reduced-model minimizer is
therefore
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
(\omega_R^{\star})^7
=\frac{9}{32}\frac{q_{\mathrm{eff}}}{m_{-4}},}
\label{eq:reduced-optimal-omega}
\end{equation}
and, with $R_S=r_S^2$,
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
r_{S,\mathrm{red}}^{\star}
=\left(\frac{32}{9}\right)^{3/7}
q_{\mathrm{eff}}^{1/14}
 m_{-4}^{3/7}T_S^{-1/2}.}
\label{eq:reduced-optimal-rs}
\end{equation}
At this stationary point the minimized error scales as
\begin{equation}
J_{\mathrm{red}}^{\star}
\propto q_{\mathrm{eff}}^{4/7}m_{-4}^{3/7}.
\label{eq:reduced-optimal-mse-scaling}
\end{equation}

Equations~\eqref{eq:reduced-optimal-omega}--\eqref{eq:reduced-optimal-rs}
are an exact minimizer of the stated reduced asymptotic cost, not an exact
solution for the complete 21-state MEKF.  The distinction is important.  The
full estimator couples attitude, gyro bias, residual accelerometer bias, and all
three translational axes through the covariance; its $S=0$ update has a full
Kalman gain; $q_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is endogenous to those coupled errors; the
pseudo-update is discrete; and the spectral-separation approximation is least
reliable during changing or multimodal seas.  Consequently the reduced
minimizer is used to understand sensitivities and to motivate a scheduler, not
to claim a universal full-filter optimum.

\subsection{Design interpretation and deployed schedule}
\label{sec:riccati-similarity-design-guidance}

The combined similarity and reduced-MSE arguments identify three useful design
ideas without uniquely determining the production coefficients.  First,
$\sigma_{aw}\tau^3$ is the natural scale of the third-integral state.  Second,
scaling the pseudo-update period with $\tau$ keeps the number of regularization
updates per OU correlation time approximately fixed.  Third, the useful
regularization strength is a drift--distortion compromise rather than covariance
matching to a true sensor.

\subsubsection{What the wrapper deploys}
\label{sec:riccati-deployed-cadence}
The deployed wrapper uses
\begin{equation}
T_S=\clip(c_T\tau_{\mathrm{appl}},T_{S,\min},T_{S,\max})
\end{equation}
and a base target $r_S^{\mathrm{base}}=c_R\sigma_{aw}\tau^3$.  It then applies
the information-rate normalization
\begin{equation}
r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}
=r_S^{\mathrm{base}}\sqrt{\frac{T_{S,0}}{T_S}},
\label{eq:deployed-rs-summary}
\end{equation}
so away from clamps the actual filter-input dependence is
$r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}\propto\sigma_{aw}\tau^{5/2}$.  The constants and
smoothing rules are given in Sec.~\ref{sec:adaptation}.  This schedule should be
read as an empirically calibrated implementation informed by the similarity and
Riccati arguments above, not as the algebraic consequence of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:reduced-optimal-rs}.

\subsubsection{Empirical amplitude check}
\label{sec:riccati-law-ablation}
The implementation exposes the amplitude dependence independently of the common
time-scale factor.  The full paired experiment finds that retaining the linear
$\sigma_{aw}$ dependence performs best among the tested amplitude exponents.
This is useful evidence for the deployed scheduler, but it does not identify a
unique physical decomposition of $q_{\mathrm{eff}}$: attitude leakage, bias
observability, model mismatch, and sensor noise all contribute to the
low-frequency acceleration error seen by the complete MEKF.  A useful
bookkeeping model, referenced by the scalar Riccati discussion above, is
\begin{equation}
R_a=R_{a,\mathrm{sensor}}+\beta_m\sigma_{aw}^2,
\label{eq:riccati-Ra-floor-plus-model}
\end{equation}
where $\beta_m$ is not a fitted sensor-noise constant but a placeholder for
sea-dependent model mismatch.  The empirical result therefore validates the
engineering law over the tested envelope rather than retroactively turning this
decomposition or the reduced model into a proof of that law.

\subsection{Horizontal channels: large drift does not imply a much tighter anchor}
\label{sec:riccati-per-axis}
\label{sec:riccati-per-axis-limits}

The horizontal and vertical channels do not have the same low-frequency error
source.  After attitude compensation, small tilt error leaks gravity into the
horizontal acceleration approximately as
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
\vct\varepsilon_{a,H}
\simeq
\begin{bmatrix}
g\,\delta\theta_y\\-g\,\delta\theta_x
\end{bmatrix}
+\vct b_{a,H}+\vct n_H .}
\label{eq:riccati-axis-tilt-leak}
\end{equation}
The vertical channel does not contain the same first-order
$g\,\delta\theta$ term.  Without GNSS or another horizontal kinematic
reference, residual horizontal bias is also less observable.  It is therefore
reasonable, and observed in the simulation diagnostics, that
$q_{\mathrm{eff},H}\gg q_{\mathrm{eff},z}$.

Across the eight stationary calibration records, the measured drift-band
intensity is about $65$ times the vertical value on world $x$ and $102$ times
on world $y$.  These are diagnostic ratios, not universal ocean constants: the
records cover only a limited set of directions, and world-axis surge/sway energy
moves with wave heading.  The important analytical result is the sensitivity,
not a fitted $x/y$ coefficient.  In the reduced model,
$q_{\mathrm{eff}}$ enters the preferred regularizer through only the
$1/14$ power.  Thus a factor-of-ten error in the drift intensity changes
$r_{S,\mathrm{red}}^{\star}$ by only
$10^{1/14}\simeq1.18$.  Even two orders of magnitude in horizontal drift is
therefore not, by itself, an argument for an order-of-magnitude stronger
horizontal $S=0$ anchor.

The desired-motion term points in the opposite direction.  For a directional
sea, $m_{-4,x}$ and $m_{-4,y}$ are primarily projections of the same orbital
motion and change with heading; strengthening one world-axis regularizer can
therefore suppress genuine wave motion rather than cure the disturbance that
made that axis noisy.  The present directional sample is too limited to infer a
universal pair of separate $x/y$ regularization constants from those moments.
The full-filter evidence supports the simpler statement actually needed by the
implementation: isotropic integral regularization performs well in the tested
directional cases.

The stronger consequence concerns engineering priority.  At the reduced-model
stationary point,
Eq.~\eqref{eq:reduced-optimal-mse-scaling} carries
$q_{\mathrm{eff}}^{4/7}$, eight times the exponent with which
$q_{\mathrm{eff}}$ enters the tuning ratio in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:reduced-optimal-rs}.  Residual horizontal error is therefore much
more sensitive to the disturbance itself than the preferred regularization
scale is.  Improving tilt accuracy, accelerometer-bias observability, or adding
a horizontal reference such as GNSS attacks the dominant quantity directly;
simply ``regularizing the noisy axis harder'' does not.

This conclusion is limited in the same way as the reduced model.  The two
orders-of-magnitude diagnostic is simulation-derived, the world-axis split is
heading dependent, and the estimator has not been tested on a broad ensemble of
crossing or multidirectional seas.  A future study should estimate the full
low-frequency acceleration-error spectral matrix and the directional
$\mat M_{-4}$ moment over a much wider directional ensemble before considering
an online anisotropic regularizer.
